INT. FLORIST'S COLD ROOM - NIGHT

Steel shelves heavy with buckets of peonies, ranunculus, white lilacs. The compressor hums. Everything is breath and mist.

IMOGEN HARTLEY-SOLANKI (30s, apron, secateurs) trims stems at a work table. DARIO FENWICK-ADEBAYO (30s, bike courier, helmet under his arm) hovers at the door. WINNIE ZHAO (60s, shop owner, quilted vest, thick glasses) counts invoices.

DARIO
I can come back.

IMOGEN
It's fine.

WINNIE
He's here for the roses.

DARIO
Two dozen. For tonight.

IMOGEN
Red?

DARIO
White.

Imogen pauses, a stem in mid-air.

IMOGEN
White. Right.

WINNIE
Dario, that's a funeral color.

DARIO
It's what she likes.

WINNIE
Who?

DARIO
(quickly)
A friend.

IMOGEN
Eighteen dollars a dozen.

DARIO
Okay.

She wraps. Her thumb catches a thorn. She doesn't flinch.

WINNIE
Imogen, you're bleeding.

IMOGEN
I'm fine.

DARIO
Do you need a--

IMOGEN
I'm fine.

Dario retrieves a tissue from his pocket and holds it out. She hesitates, then takes it. Their fingers touch.

DARIO
Hey, Imogen.

IMOGEN
Mm.

DARIO
Never mind.

She finishes the bouquet. Slides it across the table. Neither of them reaches for it.

WINNIE
(pointedly)
The roses go in his hands, dear.

Imogen lifts them. Dario takes them. The mist swirls between them. He leaves. The door clicks.

WINNIE (CONT'D)
White roses.

IMOGEN
I know.

WINNIE
He's not buying them for a friend.

IMOGEN
I know.

She lifts the next stem. The scissors snap. Winnie goes back to her invoices.
